---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_URL, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../config';

/*
  Privacy policy + terms of use. Review this when analytics, an email provider,
  or a new third-party service gets added — each changes what's true below.
*/
const updated = 'September 2026';
const email = `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy & terms"
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} collects (almost nothing) and how you can use what's here.`}
>
  <article class="pt-14 pb-8 sm:pt-20">
    <header class="wrap-prose">
      <h1 class="text-[2.25rem] sm:text-[3rem]">Privacy &amp; terms</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        The short version: nothing here tracks you, your email is only used to email
        you, and you're welcome to share what you find with credit, just not to make
        money from it.
      </p>
      <hr class="mt-8 border-rule" />
    </header>

    <div class="wrap-prose prose-note mt-8">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>

      <h3>Your email</h3>
      <p>
        If you sign up for updates, your email address is used for one thing: sending
        you updates from {SITE_NAME}. It is never sold, rented, traded or shared with
        anyone else. It's kept by the email service that sends the updates and nowhere
        else. Every email has an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing removes you. You
        can also ask to be removed at <a href={email}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>What isn't collected</h3>
      <p>
        There are no accounts or logins. No cookies are set, and there are no ads, ad
        trackers or data brokers. Nothing about you is sold, because nothing about you
        is kept.
      </p>

      <h3>The games and maps</h3>
      <p>
        They run in your browser. Your picks, filters and scores aren't sent anywhere.
        A few of them save progress (a daily streak, say) in your browser's local
        storage, on your own device. Clearing your browser data erases it.
      </p>

      <h3>Hosting and outside services</h3>
      <p>
        The site's host keeps standard server logs, including IP addresses, for
        security and to prevent abuse. Some tools load fonts or code libraries from
        outside services such as Google Fonts, which see an ordinary web request from
        your browser. Links to other sites are covered by those sites' own policies.
      </p>

      <h2>Terms of use</h2>

      <h3>Sharing what's here</h3>
      <p>
        The writing, charts, maps and graphics on {SITE_NAME} are free to share and
        adapt for non-commercial purposes, as long as you credit{" "}
        <a href={SITE_URL}>{SITE_NAME}</a> and link back to the page you used. This
        follows the <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/"
          >Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0</a
        > license. No need to ask first. A credit line like this works:
      </p>
      <p class="credit">Source: {SITE_NAME} ({SITE_URL.replace('https://', '')})</p>
      <p>
        If you reuse a chart, please also name the data source it was built on.
      </p>

      <h3>No commercial use</h3>
      <p>
        You can't sell, license or otherwise make money from anything here, or use it
        in advertising, without written permission. If you have something in mind,{" "}
        <a href={email}>ask</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Other people's data and images</h3>
      <p>
        The tools are built on data and images that belong to others: sports
        statistics, player photos, public filings. Those aren't mine to license, and
        the terms above don't cover them. Each tool's methods note names its sources,
        and reusing that material means following their terms. Campaign-finance data
        from the Federal Election Commission, for example, can't be used to solicit
        contributions or for any commercial purpose.
      </p>

      <h3>No guarantees</h3>
      <p>
        Everything here is provided as-is, for information and fun. The numbers are
        checked, but errors happen. Don't rely on them for financial, legal or betting
        decisions. If you find a mistake, <a href={email}>let me know</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        Last updated {updated}. If any of this changes in a way that matters, it'll be
        noted here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .credit {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-rule);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
